WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.642
When a C program launches another process,

2
00:00:02.722 --> 00:00:06.767
the child may inherit environment
variables from the parent unless the launch is

3
00:00:06.847 --> 00:00:07.918
designed otherwise.

4
00:00:07.998 --> 00:00:09.961
This is convenient for ordinary tools,

5
00:00:10.041 --> 00:00:14.086
but it can be unsafe for sensitive
workflows because the child may receive values

6
00:00:14.166 --> 00:00:15.951
that were meant only for the parent.

7
00:00:16.031 --> 00:00:20.136
Inherited environment state can include
configuration, secrets, search paths,

8
00:00:20.216 --> 00:00:24.142
locale settings, debugging flags, runtime
options, temporary directories,

9
00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:25.174
and feature modes.

10
00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:27.931
Some of those values may change how the
child behaves.

11
00:00:28.011 --> 00:00:31.343
Others may simply expose information that
the child does not need.

12
00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:33.148
Both outcomes deserve attention.

13
00:00:33.228 --> 00:00:37.571
Process launch code should define the
child environment explicitly when the workflow

14
00:00:37.651 --> 00:00:38.365
is sensitive.

15
00:00:38.445 --> 00:00:40.586
Instead of passing everything by default,

16
00:00:40.666 --> 00:00:44.533
build a small environment containing the
variables that are part of the child

17
00:00:44.613 --> 00:00:45.565
process contract.

18
00:00:45.645 --> 00:00:49.809
That contract should be documented so
operators and reviewers understand what the

19
00:00:49.889 --> 00:00:50.901
child needs and why.

20
00:00:50.981 --> 00:00:55.026
Unsafe inherited values should be cleared
or replaced before launching tools or

21
00:00:55.106 --> 00:00:55.582
helpers.

22
00:00:55.662 --> 00:00:58.636
The specific list depends on the platform
and application,

23
00:00:58.716 --> 00:01:00.382
but the principle is consistent:

24
00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:04.626
do not let unrelated parent state
influence a child operation that is supposed to be

25
00:01:04.706 --> 00:01:05.361
controlled.

26
00:01:05.441 --> 00:01:07.701
This discipline also improves reliability.

27
00:01:07.781 --> 00:01:11.708
A child process that receives only
expected variables is easier to reproduce,

28
00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:13.037
test, and troubleshoot.

29
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:17.162
It behaves less like a product of the
operator's shell or service wrapper and more

30
00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:19.860
like a defined component in the
application design.
